rkdown or `<img>` form, found with the existing `extract_github_inline_image_urls`), it does four things. First, it renders `![image](<url>)` with `client.render_markdown` and reads the src of the first `<img>` (HTML-unescaped). Second, it downloads that signed URL with `client.download` and rejects any Content-Type that is not `image/*`. Third, it takes the file name from Content-Disposition (base name only), or uses `github-attachment-<id><ext>` when there is none. Fourth, it creates the `Attachment` as specified, saves the file with `attached_file.save`, and returns `<file url>#<refresh fragment>`. If any step fails for one image, the original link stays, `logger.warning` is called, and the next image is processed. Both `_import_user_stories_data` and `_import_issues_data` call it after the object is created, through the helper `_import_description_inline_images`. The description is saved only if it changed, and `obj.description` is also updated so the first history snapshot shows the local URLs. Results: 7 new integration tests pass, `make check-fast` passes (233), and the full suite passes (1753 passed in 13 min 49 s). I ran the full suite with pytest and a separate test database, because `make test` failed in `db-up`: port 5432 is used by another worktree's Postgres. Nothing was committed.\",\"changes\":[{\"path\":\"taiga/importers/github/importer.py\",\"summary\":\"Module `logger` and imports for `Attachment`, `ContentType` and the attachments services. Helpers for URL replacement, reading the first img src, case-insensitive header lookup and Content-Disposition file names. New methods `_import_inline_images`, `_import_inline_image` and `_import_description_inline_images`, called in both import loops after the object is created.\"},{\"path\":\"tests/integration/test_importers_github_inline_images.py\",\"summary\":\"7 tests: URLs rewritten to local attachments with the refresh fragment, a repeated URL imported once, `<img>` form, attachment fields, fallback fi